=head1 NAME

fs_pathinfo - Shows data about every component of a path

=head1 SYNOPSIS

=for html
<div class="synopsis">

B<fs pathinfo> S<<< [B<-path> <I<dir/file path>>+] >>> [B<-verbose>] [B<-help>]

=for html
</div>

=head1 DESCRIPTION

The fs pathinfo command displays the type of every component of a
path. If a mount point, it shows which volume it references. If a symlink,
it shows the target path or the volume it resolves to.

This command walks in a given path, and resolves each prefix using the
pioctl interface. It avoids running B<fs examine> and B<fs lsmount> on
each component.

The root directory C</> is never described: the walk begins at the first
component below it, so a path in AFS is reported from C</afs> down.

A C<.> or C<..> component names no entry of its own, so it is interpreted
instead of being described: the components that follow it are reported
under the path they are really reached by. The same is done for the leading
C<.> and C<..> of a symbolic link target. When a path ends in such a
component, the directory it leads to is described, so that the last line
reported is the entry the path names, unless that entry is the root
directory.

=head1 CAUTIONS

If a component doesn't exist, the command displays "<component> does not
exist" and stops walking that path; any remaining B<-path> arguments are
still processed.

If the command is used outside AFS, it will walk the path and display the type
of each entry.

A path that names the root directory itself, such as C</> or C</afs/..>,
has nothing left to report once the root is left out, so no line is
displayed for it.

=head1 OPTIONS

=over 4

=item B<-path> <I<dir/file path>>+

Names each component for which to display information about
itself. Partial pathnames are interpreted relative to the
current working directory, which is also the default value if this
argument is omitted. Only the components named on the command line are
described; the current working directory is taken as given, unless the
B<-verbose> argument is provided.

=item B<-verbose>

Describes a partial pathname in full, starting at the topmost component:
the components of the current working directory are described as well as
those named by the B<-path> argument.

The path a symbolic link leads to is described in full as well, starting
again at its topmost component, rather than being reported as a single
component. A blank line separates each of these from what came before it.
Should a component of that path be a symbolic link in its turn, it is
reported along with what it leads to, but the path it leads to is not
described again from its topmost component. Without this argument only the
entry the link leads to is described.

=item B<-help>

Prints the online help for this command. All other valid options are
ignored.

=back

=head1 OUTPUT

The output displays information about every component of a path,
showing the type of items (mount point, symlink, directory, file)
in the following format:

    'component' is a (mount point|symbolic link|directory|regular file|special file), <additional information corresponding to the type>

One line like this appears per component in the path. A symbolic link
names the path it leads to, and that path is described on a line of its
own:

    'component' is a symbolic link, leading to 'target'
    'target' is a ...

=head1 EXAMPLES

The pathinfo command can take multiple paths:

    % fs pathinfo path1 path2 ...

The following example shows a full path walk of the command on
multiple types of items:

    % fs pathinfo -path /afs/example.com/usr/smith/joeSymlink/file
    '/afs' is a directory
    '/afs/example.com' is a mount point, for volume '#example.com:root.cell'
    '/afs/example.com/usr' is a mount point, for volume '#usr'
    '/afs/example.com/usr/smith' is a directory
    '/afs/example.com/usr/smith/joeSymlink' is a symbolic link, leading to '/afs/example.com/usr/joe'
    '/afs/example.com/usr/joe' is a mount point, for volume '#joe'
    '/afs/example.com/usr/joe/file' is a regular file

The following example shows the command used on a normal path:

    % fs pathinfo /tmp/foo/bar
    '/tmp' is a directory
    '/tmp/foo' is a directory
    '/tmp/foo/bar' is a regular file

The following example shows a partial pathname, which describes only the
components that were named:

    % cd /afs/example.com/usr/smith
    % fs pathinfo joeSymlink/file
    '/afs/example.com/usr/smith/joeSymlink' is a symbolic link, leading to '/afs/example.com/usr/joe'
    '/afs/example.com/usr/joe' is a mount point, for volume '#joe'
    '/afs/example.com/usr/joe/file' is a regular file

The following example shows a partial pathname leading out of the current
working directory; the C<..> is interpreted, and what follows it is
reported under the path it is reached by:

    % cd /afs/example.com/usr/smith
    % fs pathinfo ../joe/file
    '/afs/example.com/usr/joe' is a mount point, for volume '#joe'
    '/afs/example.com/usr/joe/file' is a regular file

The same partial pathname with the B<-verbose> argument, which describes
the current working directory as well, and describes the path the symbolic
link leads to from its topmost component down:

    % fs pathinfo -path joeSymlink/file -verbose
    '/afs' is a directory
    '/afs/example.com' is a mount point, for volume '#example.com:root.cell'
    '/afs/example.com/usr' is a mount point, for volume '#usr'
    '/afs/example.com/usr/smith' is a directory
    '/afs/example.com/usr/smith/joeSymlink' is a symbolic link, leading to '/afs/example.com/usr/joe'

    '/afs' is a directory
    '/afs/example.com' is a mount point, for volume '#example.com:root.cell'
    '/afs/example.com/usr' is a mount point, for volume '#usr'
    '/afs/example.com/usr/joe' is a mount point, for volume '#joe'
    '/afs/example.com/usr/joe/file' is a regular file

=head1 PRIVILEGE REQUIRED

The issuer must have the C<l> (list) permission on the ACL of each directory
that precedes the file or directory named by the B<-path> argument.

